Module 27: Instagram Security, Attacks & Prevention (Ethical Perspective)
1. Why Instagram is a Target

Instagram accounts are targeted because:

Personal data

Business pages & ads

Influencer money

Brand reputation

Direct messages & contacts

Most attacks succeed due to human mistakes, not technical flaws.

2. Common Instagram Attack Methods (Awareness Only)
1️⃣ Phishing Attacks

Most common method.

How it works:

Fake login pages

Fake “copyright claim”

Fake “account verification”

Fake “blue tick” messages

Victim enters credentials voluntarily.

🚩 Red flag: “Your account will be disabled in 24 hours”

2️⃣ Fake Emails & DMs

Attackers pretend to be:

Instagram support

Meta team

Verified services

They send:

Fake reset links

Malicious URLs

3️⃣ Credential Stuffing

Uses leaked passwords from other websites

Works if user reuses passwords

Example:

Same password on Facebook + Instagram = risk

4️⃣ Brute Force Attempts

Automated login attempts

Usually blocked by Instagram

Works only on very weak passwords

5️⃣ Third‑Party App Abuse

“Followers booster”

“Story viewers”

“Analytics apps”

These apps:

Ask for login access

Steal sessions or credentials

6️⃣ Session Hijacking

Stealing active login sessions

Happens via:

Malware

Infected browsers

Unsafe devices

3. Social Engineering on Instagram

Instagram attacks rely heavily on:

Trust

Urgency

Curiosity

Authority

Examples:

“Is this you in this video?”

“You violated copyright”

“Get verified today”

4. Signs Your Account is Compromised

🚩 Password changed
🚩 Unknown login alerts
🚩 Emails changed
🚩 Suspicious DMs sent
🚩 Posts you didn’t upload
🚩 New followers you didn’t add

5. How Instagram Protects Accounts

Instagram uses:

Login rate limiting

Suspicious login detection

Device fingerprinting

IP reputation

Two‑factor authentication (2FA)

This makes direct hacking extremely difficult.

6. How to Secure an Instagram Account (Very Important)
Strong Password

✔ Unique
✔ Long
✔ Never reused

Enable 2FA

Best options:

Authenticator app

Backup codes

This alone stops 90% of attacks.

Check Login Activity

Review devices & locations

Log out unknown sessions

Remove Suspicious Apps

Disconnect third‑party apps

Avoid “free followers” tools

Secure Email First

If email is hacked → Instagram is gone.

7. Instagram Recovery (If Hacked)

Steps (high-level):

Use “Secure your account”

Reset password

Verify identity

Remove attacker email

Enable 2FA

8. Defensive & Blue Team View

Organizations protect social media by:

Awareness training

MFA enforcement

Monitoring account activity

Incident response plans

9. Ethical Hacking Perspective

In cybersecurity:

We study attacks to prevent them

We never exploit real users

Social media security = defensive skill
